# Build output
[Bb]in/
[Oo]bj/
[Oo]ut/
[Dd]ebug/
[Rr]elease/
artifacts/
publish/
*.dll
*.exe
*.pdb

# .NET / VS / Rider / editors
.vs/
.vscode/
.idea/
.obsidian/
*.user
*.suo
*.userosscache
*.sln.docstates
*.received.*
project.lock.json
project.fragment.lock.json

# Test + coverage
[Tt]est[Rr]esult*/
coverage*.json
coverage*.xml
coverage*.info
*.trx
*.coverage
*.coveragexml
BenchmarkDotNet.Artifacts/

# NuGet
*.nupkg
*.snupkg
packages/
!packages/build/

# Local secrets / machine-specific config.
# NOTE: this app is local-first and stores no credentials, but never let a
# captured provider payload or settings file with machine paths be committed.
*.local.json
appsettings.Development.json
secrets.json

# Captured provider fixtures that were not manually redacted.
# Redacted fixtures belong in tests and SHOULD be committed.
**/fixtures/raw/
*.raw.json

# OS noise
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/
*.lnk

# Git Bash / MSYS crash artifacts. These reappear in the working tree whenever
# a bash subprocess faults; they carry no project value and must never land in
# a commit.
*.stackdump

# Process documents. These stay on disk and are used every day locally; they are
# deliberately not part of the public tree. The repository is a product, not a
# record of how it was built — the plans, specs, design output and PRD say more
# about the workflow than about the application, and the design folder alone is
# ~500 KB of generated HTML plus a vendored component runtime.
#
# They remain readable in this repository's history; untracking them stops them
# accumulating in HEAD, it does not unpublish them.
/CLAUDE.md
/AGENTS.md
docs/PRD.md
docs/codex-workflow.md
docs/pre-publication-checklist.md
docs/design/
docs/plans/
docs/specs/
